<section class="page-hero">
  <div id="ambient-canvas" class="ambient-canvas"></div>
  <div class="container page-hero-inner">
    <span class="eyebrow">Free Tool</span>
    <h1>Free Robots.txt Tester — Check If a URL Is Allowed or Blocked</h1>
    <p>Paste your robots.txt, choose a crawler and list the URLs you want to check. This <strong>free robots.txt tester</strong> applies Google's matching rules, shows which rule decided each URL and flags lines Google ignores. No signup, nothing leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container contact-grid llms-tool-grid">
    <div>
      <form class="contact-form llms-form" id="rt-form" onsubmit="return false;" aria-label="Robots.txt tester form">
        <div>
          <label for="rt-file">Your robots.txt (paste the contents)</label>
          <textarea id="rt-file" style="min-height: 200px;" placeholder="User-agent: *&#10;Disallow: /admin&#10;Disallow: /search?&#10;Allow: /admin/help&#10;&#10;User-agent: GPTBot&#10;Disallow: /&#10;&#10;Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="rt-ua">Crawler to test</label>
          <input type="text" id="rt-ua" list="rt-ua-list" value="Googlebot" autocomplete="off">
          <datalist id="rt-ua-list">
            <option value="Googlebot"></option><option value="Googlebot-Image"></option><option value="Bingbot"></option><option value="GPTBot"></option><option value="ClaudeBot"></option><option value="PerplexityBot"></option><option value="*"></option>
          </datalist>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="rt-urls">URLs or paths to check (one per line)</label>
          <textarea id="rt-urls" style="min-height: 120px;" placeholder="https://example.com/admin/settings&#10;/admin/help/faq&#10;/blog/my-post&#10;/search?q=shoes"></textarea>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>

    <div class="contact-info-card llms-preview-card">
      <div class="llms-preview-head">
        <strong>Test results</strong>
        <span id="rt-status" class="llms-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Waiting for input</span>
      </div>
      <pre id="rt-output" class="llms-output" style="max-height:420px;white-space:pre-wrap;">Paste a robots.txt and add URLs to test.</pre>
      <div class="llms-actions" style="margin-top:14px;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" id="rt-copy">Copy</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer">

    <h2>How the Tester Decides</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>One group applies.</strong> Google uses the group whose user agent is the most specific match for the crawler. Rules from groups for the same agent are combined, and the <code>*</code> group is used only when no specific group matches.</li>
      <li><strong>The longest matching rule wins.</strong> Each Allow or Disallow path is compared with the start of the URL path, and the most specific rule, judged by path length, decides.</li>
      <li><strong>A tie goes to Allow.</strong> When an Allow and a Disallow match with the same length, Google uses the least restrictive rule.</li>
      <li><strong>Wildcards.</strong> <code>*</code> matches any run of characters and <code>$</code> marks the end of the URL.</li>
      <li><strong>Paths are case-sensitive.</strong> <code>/Admin</code> and <code>/admin</code> are different rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Empty Disallow blocks nothing.</strong> A rule with no path is ignored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a Robots.txt Test Can and Cannot Tell You</h2>
    <p>The result tells you whether a crawler that follows these rules may fetch a URL. It does not tell you whether the page is indexed. A URL blocked by robots.txt can still appear in search results if other pages link to it, and a page you allow may still be left out of the index for other reasons. If the page must stay out of search, a noindex rule on a crawlable page is the right tool.</p>
    <p>The tool follows the rules Google documents. Other crawlers can differ in small ways, so treat a result for a non-Google crawler as a close guide, and check that crawler's own documentation if the outcome matters. Google also supports only the user-agent, allow, disallow and sitemap fields and ignores others, such as crawl-delay, and it enforces a size limit of 500 KiB on the file.</p>

    <h2>Robots.txt Tester FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Does the tester fetch my live robots.txt?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Paste the contents. Browsers block most cross-site requests to other people's files, and pasting keeps your data off every server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which rule wins when Allow and Disallow both match?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>The longer, more specific path wins. If both are the same length, Google uses the least restrictive rule, which is Allow.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does my URL show as allowed when I have a Disallow for its folder?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>A longer Allow rule may be overriding it, or the rule may sit in a group for a different crawler. The result lists the rule that decided each URL so you can see which one it was.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I test a crawler like GPTBot or ClaudeBot?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. Type the crawler's name in the crawler field. If your file has no group for it, the tester falls back to the <code>*</code> group, as Google does.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. The rules are applied in your browser with JavaScript — nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-band" style="margin-top: 44px;">
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-1"></span>
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-2"></span>
      <h2>Not sure what your robots.txt should block?</h2>
      <p>Contomatix reviews crawl rules as part of a technical SEO check.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get in touch</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/<%= site.whatsappNumber %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-glass">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

